title: Revert
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: overview
aliases: revert
summary: A revert undoes one past write or operation in place — writing back the state it replaced as a new write — while everything after it stays.

Rollback moves HEAD: it undoes everything back to a point in time, and what it passes
over is undone because it is ''in the way'', not because anyone objected to it. Right for
"take me back to lunchtime"; wrong for "that one rename was a mistake" — above all in a
watched repository, where dozens of unrelated watcher writes may have landed since.

A ''revert'' answers the second question. It undoes one write, or one operation,
''without moving HEAD'': the daemon computes the state that change replaced and writes it
back as an ordinary new write. The log grows by one entry saying so, and nothing
leaves it — the mistake and its correction are both on the record, which is what an
audit log is for.

A revert is an ordinary write in every other respect: it can itself be reverted
(restoring the original change), rolled back across, pruned and trimmed. What marks it is
`reverts_op_id` on each of its operations, naming what it undid.

A revert goes back to what the change replaced, whatever was written since on the same
field: a later write is overwritten, and the plan says so first ([[Reverting onto the current state]]). Undo reverts for you when the watcher has written since your change
([[Undo and redo]]).

